{"name":"Corn Husk Green","hex":"#cecd95","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"corn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any cereal plant (or its grain) that is the main crop or staple of a country or region.","Maize, a grain crop of the species Zea mays."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To granulate; to form (a substance) into grains.","To preserve using coarse salt, e.g. corned beef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/corn"},{"term":"husk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dry, leafy or stringy exterior of certain vegetables or fruits, which must be removed before eating the meat inside.","Any form of useless, dried up, and subsequently worthless exterior of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove husks from."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/husk"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Husk","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a husk is the outer shell or coating of a seed. In the United States, the term husk often refers to the leafy outer covering of an ear of maize (corn) as it grows on the plant. Literally, a husk or hull includes the protective outer covering of a seed, fruit, or vegetable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Involucre_d%27un_noisetier_de_Bysance.jpg/330px-Involucre_d%27un_noisetier_de_Bysance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Husk","wikidata":"Q14524280"},{"title":"Maize","blocks":[{"text":"Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize","wikidata":"Q11575"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Maize (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The shade maize or corn refers to a specific tone of yellow; it is named for the cereal of the same name—maize. In public usage, maize can be applied to a variety of shades, ranging from light yellow to a dark shade that borders on orange, since the color of maize may vary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Cornheap.jpg/330px-Cornheap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_(color)","wikidata":"Q3843568"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg/500px-E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg/1280px-E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg/500px-E8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Corn being husked in the yard of a Dungan farmer in Kyrgyzstan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AE8025-Milyanfan-corn-huskers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg/500px-Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg/1280px-Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg/500px-Africa_Food_Security_11_%2810665081134%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A woman manually dehusking corn in Malawi. People working in subsistence agricultural settings tend not to have the mechanization of processing practices to reduce the labor. In many societies, this labor falls to family members such as women, who make up the majority of farmers working in smallholdings.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAfrica_Food_Security_11_(10665081134).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoeh-283.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Corntassel_7095.jpg/250px-Corntassel_7095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Corntassel_7095.jpg/330px-Corntassel_7095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Corntassel_7095.jpg/250px-Corntassel_7095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACorntassel_7095.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Cornsilk_7091.jpg/250px-Cornsilk_7091.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Cornsilk_7091.jpg/330px-Cornsilk_7091.jpg?utm_source=e